{"post": {"ID": 426, "post_title": "Zero Click Searches: What They Are and How to Win Anyway", "post_excerpt": "What zero click searches are, how common they are in three studies, whether they hurt SEO, how to be the source of the answer, how to measure value without clicks and a worked example.", "post_author": "3", "post_date": "2025-01-26 15:26:57", "post_modified": "2026-11-15 08:43:58", "post_content_b64": ""}, "meta": {"_thumbnail_id": "633", "_yoast_wpseo_title": "Zero Click Searches: How to Win Anyway | Reinforce Lab", "_yoast_wpseo_metadesc": "Zero click searches explained: how common they are in three studies, whether they hurt SEO, how to be the source of the answer and how to measure value.", "rl_updated": "2026-11-15", "_rl_carried": "a:3:{s:7:\"prod_id\";i:215527;s:4:\"from\";s:58:\"https://reinforcelab.com/how-to-avoid-zero-click-searches/\";s:8:\"decision\";s:5:\"D-178\";}", "rl_type": "guide", "_yoast_wpseo_primary_category": "12", "_yoast_wpseo_focuskw": "zero click searches", "rl_answer": "A zero click search is a search that ends without a click on any result. Roughly 58.5% of US Google searches ended that way in SparkToro's 2024 study, and AI summaries make it more likely. You cannot avoid them. Be the source of the answer, give people a reason to visit, aim at searches where a visit pays, build your name across the web and measure impressions, mentions and leads.", "rl_faqs": "What is a zero click search? | A search that ends without the person clicking any result. It includes searches answered on the page, searches where the person gave up and searches where they changed the query. SparkToro defines it as ending without clicking any presented result.\nHow common are zero click searches? | SparkToro and Datos found 58.5% of US and 59.7% of EU Google searches had zero clicks between September 2022 and May 2024. Pew and Bain report similar direction with different methods. Numbers vary by method, country and query type.\nDo zero click searches hurt SEO? | They reduce clicks on simple questions and change what SEO is worth. Buying and comparison searches still produce visits, and being named in an answer can add awareness. Check which of your pages depend on clicks from simple queries.\nAre zero click searches bad for users? | Often not: a quick answer saves time for simple questions. They are a problem when the answer is wrong, thin or hides its source. OpenAI says ChatGPT search results and citations can be incomplete, outdated or incorrect, so open a source when accuracy matters.\nHow do AI Overviews affect clicks? | Pew tracked 900 US adults in March 2025: people clicked a regular result in 8% of visits with an AI summary and 15% without, and clicked a link inside the summary in 1% of visits. Google disputes the study's method.\nHow do you optimize for zero click searches? | Put a clear answer in the first sentence under a question heading, keep facts current and sourced, make pages indexable and snippet-eligible, add something worth a visit, and build mentions on other sites. No one can guarantee a snippet or AI citation.\nIs there value in zero click searches? | Yes. Being named in an answer can raise branded search and direct visits, and local answers such as map packs bring calls and direction requests. Measure impressions, branded search, mentions and leads, not clicks alone.\nHow do you measure zero click performance? | Track impressions and click-through rate in Search Console, Google's Generative AI performance report, branded search, mentions and citations across a fixed prompt list, and leads or calls by source with a how-did-you-hear question.\nWhen should you not chase zero click visibility? | When a page only gives away something you earn from per click and you cannot benefit from awareness. It is worth chasing when buyers who see your name later contact you, or when you need to be known in a market.\nCan you avoid zero click searches? | No. You cannot control what Google shows. Aim to be the source of the answer, give people a reason to visit and target searches where a visit creates value.", "rl_sources": "SparkToro: 2024 zero-click search study, for every 1,000 US Google searches only 374 clicks go to the open web | https://sparktoro.com/blog/2024-zero-click-search-study-for-every-1000-us-google-searches-only-374-clicks-go-to-the-open-web-in-the-eu-its-360/\nSearch Engine Land: Nearly 60% of Google searches end without a click in 2024 (July 2024) | https://searchengineland.com/google-search-zero-click-study-2024-443869\nPew Research Center: Google users are less likely to click on links when an AI summary appears (July 2025) | https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/07/22/google-users-are-less-likely-to-click-on-links-when-an-ai-summary-appears-in-the-results/\nBain and Company: Goodbye clicks, hello AI, zero-click search redefines marketing (February 2025) | https://www.bain.com/insights/goodbye-clicks-hello-ai-zero-click-search-redefines-marketing/\nSparkToro: AIs are highly inconsistent when recommending brands or products (2026) | https://sparktoro.com/blog/new-research-ais-are-highly-inconsistent-when-recommending-brands-or-products-marketers-should-take-care-when-tracking-ai-visibility/\nGoogle Search Central: AI features and your website | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features\nGoogle Search Console Help: Generative AI performance report (Search) | https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/16984139\nOpenAI Help Center: Searching the web with ChatGPT | https://help.openai.com/en/articles/9237897-searching-the-web-with-chatgpt\nGoogle Search Central: Spam policies for Google web search | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies", "rl_takeaways": "A zero click search ends without a click on any result. It may mean the person was satisfied, frustrated or changed the query.\nSparkToro and Datos (2024): 58.5% of US and 59.7% of EU Google searches had zero clicks. The panel has limits and likely undercounts.\nPew (2025): people clicked a regular result in 8% of visits with an AI summary and 15% without, and 1% inside the summary. Google disputes the method.\nBain (Dec 2024, 1,117 consumers): about 80% rely on zero click results in at least 40% of searches. Bain estimates organic traffic falls 15% to 25%.\nYou cannot avoid zero click searches. Be the source, give people a reason to visit, and target searches where a visit pays.\nMeasure impressions, Google's Generative AI performance report, branded search, mentions and leads, not clicks alone.\nDecide page by page what an appearance and a visit are worth.", "rl_g_glossary": "Zero click search | A search that ends without a click on any result.\nFeatured snippet | An answer box that Google shows at the top of some results.\nAI Overview | A short AI-written summary at the top of some Google results, with links.\nClickstream data | Records of the pages people visit and the clicks they make, collected from a panel.\nBranded search | A search that includes your company or product name.\nImpression | One showing of your page in search results, whether or not it is clicked.", "rl_g_cta_service": "services/ai-search-optimization", "rl_g_cta_head": "Want to be the source of the answer?", "rl_g_cta_sub": "Reinforce Lab starts with a free diagnostic of your Google and AI search visibility and a 90-day plan.", "rl_g_cta_button": "See AI search optimization"}}